--TEST--
A first-class callable over a member declaring `inout` or a variadic tail is refused — `rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type` gives `callable` no parameter list, so nothing at the call could stage a cell or collect a tail
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:types/callable-values` gives the `(...)` spelling to a member, and `rule:types/callable-is-the-only-function-type`
// gives the `callable` it produces no parameter list. So a call through one
// passes what it was written with: there is no site that could know to hand
// `bump` an address rather than an `int`, and the callee reads that slot at
// the declared representation either way. That is a type confusion in the
// callee's own frame, not a wrong answer, so it is refused where it is
// written.
class Adder {
    public static function bump(inout int $n): void {
        $n = $n + 1;
    }

    public static function total(int ...$parts): uint {
        return Core\Arr::count($parts);
    }
}

callable $bump = Adder::bump(...);
callable $total = Adder::total(...);
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0793]: `Adder::bump` has no first-class callable form
%A
error[E0793]: `Adder::total` has no first-class callable form
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
